AB  - Large language model (LLM) agents are increasingly expected to operate external tools, browse information, edit files, execute code, and deliver task artifacts. However, many practical tasks are not single-turn question-answering problems; they require multi-step planning, tool invocation, intermediate-state management, failure recovery, and result verification. This paper presents OpenClaw-Bench, a reproducible experimental protocol for evaluating governed multi-agent harnesses on multi-step tool-use tasks. The protocol is motivated by OpenClaw, a Supervisor-Worker runtime designed to make long-horizon tool use more governable without repeating implementation details in the abstract. We define a benchmark covering five task families: file and data processing, code repair, evidence-grounded web research, document generation, and cross-tool workflows. The proposed evaluation compares a single-agent tool user, a naive multi-agent baseline, the full OpenClaw harness, and five ablated variants. Metrics combine automated checks, LLM-as-a-judge scoring, human calibration, execution-cost accounting, tool-use correctness, collaboration quality, and error recovery. By formalizing task cards, system variants, scoring dimensions, and statistical procedures, OpenClaw-Bench provides an implementation-ready protocol for studying reliability in long-horizon tool-use tasks.
ER  - 

