AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is a widely used framework for circumventing the knowledge constraints of LLMs. General-purpose RAG systems are naive to domain-specific terminology and structure. As a result, prior work such as OG-RAG leverages ontologies to bake expert knowledge and domain understanding into RAG systems. However, the feasibility, effectiveness, and accuracy of OG-RAG is completely dependent on the ontology. In order to build new ontologies and modify existing ontologies for OG-RAG, it is critical to understand how structural aspects of an ontology impact performance. In this study, we systematically evaluate how the structure of an ontology determines performance across two key tasks: document-to-ontology mapping, and question-answering. To measure its performance, we applied OG-RAG across two specialized domains: chronic lower back pain and spinal cord injury rehabilitation. We measured how the specificity of the ontology classes can impact the performance of OG-RAG, finding that an ontology containing more general classes can reduce the risk of hallucinations during document-to-ontology mapping, while using specific instance-level classes can hinder accuracy. Furthermore, we investigate the impact of adding class descriptions on OG-RAG behavior, revealing an increase in performance when providing the additional context. Our results identify potential best practices when building ontologies for OG-RAG, enabling its application across domains.
ER  - 

