AB  - Digital mental-health systems are increasingly deployed as first-line support for stress, anxiety, and mood disorders, yet many conversational agents still lack reliable emotionawareness, safety mechanisms, and clinically grounded escalation paths. This paper presents an Emotion-Aware Digital Therapy Assistant that combines large language models (LLMs), multimodal emotion detection, and agentic workflows to deliver context-sensitive and safety-conscious mental-wellness support. The assistant first estimates the user’s affective state from text and conversational context, then evaluates psychological risk using a healthcare-tuned LLM and rule-based safety graph, and finally chooses between therapeutic conversation, coping exercises, or escalation to emergency workflows (e.g., crisis alerts, human clinicians). We design a realistic benchmark—EMOThera—comprising 8,500 annotated utterances across seven emotion categories and three risk levels and report results on emotion classification, crisis detection, latency, and user experience. The proposed system achieves 89.7% macro accuracy and 0.88 macro F1 for emotion recognition, 0.91 AUC for high-risk detection, and a median end-to-end latency of 430ms per turn on commodity hardware, while 81.3% of participants in a small pilot (n = 32) report feeling "emotionally understood". These findings suggest that combining LLMs with structured safety workflows and lightweight multimodal modeling can yield an accessible, low-friction, and safer pathway between self-help tools and professional mental-health care.
ER  - 

