AB  - Abstractive summarization methods can produce fluent, readable summaries, but they do not always remain fully grounded in the source document. Previous work in this domain has repeatedly highlighted these factual inconsistencies and explored various ways to mitigate them. It includes reinforcement learning, contrastive objectives, and semantic modelling approaches. Despite these efforts, maintaining factual accuracy remains difficult, especially with long and information-rich inputs. To address this challenge, we introduce an agentic generation framework that divides the summarization process into four stages: planning, constrained generation, verification, and iterative refinement. This framework functions through a modular pipeline. First it identifies key factual units from the source. Then it generates structured summaries and evaluates their consistency using entailment-and question–answering-based signals. Finally, it refines the output to correct any detected inconsistencies. We assess our proposed model on standard benchmarks, including CNN/Daily Mail, Multi-News, WikiSum and DUC, using both ROUGE and factuality-focused evaluation metrics. The experimental results indicate consistent improvements in factual accuracy with ROUGE scores of R1 (45.5), R2 (22.8) and RL (42.6) achieved on CNN/Daily Mail, as well as average FactCC (0.86) and average QA-based score (0.84). Ablation analysis further demonstrates that each stage plays a significant role in the overall performance. These findings suggest that agentic summarization offers an effective approach to enhance the reliability of real-world summarization systems.
ER  - 

