AB  - The meteoric increase in popularity of video-based e-learning platforms has made learning more accessible; nevertheless, due chiefly to their passive nature, these platforms fail to foster interactivity between learners themselves, actual contextualization of information, and retention. In this paper, we present a multimodal generative AI learning assistant based on interactivity and personalized learning from videos. The working framework comprises automatic speech recognition for extracting transcripts aligned with video over time, transformer models for embedding-based semantic representation, and FAISS-based retrieval from an indexed corpus of dense vector representations supporting retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). Multimodal content understanding gets realized by the application of optical character recognition and computer vision for extracting textual and visual information from lecture slides and diagrams. An interactive chatbot built using a large language model provides context-aware question answering using retrieved video segments tied to the exact timestamps. The system was built on scalable web architecture, with React.js as a front-end technology, Python Flask/FastAPI service as a back end for processing, and a cloud-hosted vector database for efficient indexing and retrieval. Experimental evaluation based on retrieval relevance and response accuracy metrics proves reduced information access time and increased learning efficiency. The proposed system converts the passive consumption of videos into an active intelligent and learner-centered educational experience.
