AB  - The massive increase in digital documents like research papers, reports, contracts, and scanned records has rendered the process of analyzing documents manually to be more and more time-consuming and inefficient. Traditional document readers are mainly associated with searching using keywords and provide little assistance in the knowledge of document organization, numerical information, or semantic context. Despite the recent developments of Large Language Models (LLM), that allow engaging with documents through dialogue, a significant number of systems do not provide reliable support of the structured extraction, visualization and scanned document processing. In this paper, we introduce InsightScan, a single document analysis and interaction system that allows us to understand documents in a structured way, perform automated visualization and document-grounded question answering with a single system. The proposed method converts unstructured and semi-structured documents into a structured intermediate form, which is always re-purposed to provide analysis visualization and conversational queries. Such disintersection of extraction and reasoning enhances reliability, interpretability and consistency of data. InsightScan is a combination of text extraction via OCR, structured normalization, automatic charting and a natural language interaction web-based interface. Financial records are taken as representative evaluation examples because of their complexity in terms of structure and numerical aspects. Through experimentation, it has been found that the system yields correct structured outputs, viable visualizations and grounded conversational reactions without any numerical hallucination. InsightScan allows users to go through documents visually and through a conversational system, thus eliminating the manual work and enhancing efficiency of analysis. The suggested framework illustrates the viability of the general-purpose, single-document interaction system that may be applicable to research, scholarly education, and data-driven decision-making processes.
ER  - 

